=== Finite memory (only 3 states of counting) ===
  'ab'         accept  (counted 1 a's, 1 b's)
  'aabb'       accept  (counted 2 a's, 2 b's)
  'aaabbb'     accept  (counted 3 a's, 3 b's)
  'aaaabbbb'   REJECT  (ran out of states after 3 a's)
  'aab'        REJECT  (counted 2 a's, 1 b's)
  'abb'        REJECT  (counted 1 a's, 2 b's)
  'ba'         REJECT  (unexpected symbol)

=== Unbounded stack ===
  'ab'         accept  (stack ended empty)
  'aabb'       accept  (stack ended empty)
  'aaabbb'     accept  (stack ended empty)
  'aaaabbbb'   accept  (stack ended empty)
  'aab'        REJECT  (stack ended non-empty)
  'abb'        REJECT  (a 'b' with nothing to match)
  'ba'         REJECT  (a 'b' with nothing to match)

=== Watch the stack do the counting on aaaabbbb ===
        stack after each symbol: a | aa | aaa | aaaa | aaa | aa | a | (empty)

Notice WHERE they diverge: 'aaaabbbb' is a perfectly good member of
the language, and the finite machine rejects it only because it ran
out of states. Raise MAX_STATES to 4 and it works... until n = 5.
No FIXED number of states works for ALL n. That is the Type 3 ceiling.
